From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 08:45:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] win32u, gdi32, wow64win: D3DKMT keyed mutexes: Create, Open,
 Destroy, Acquire, Release (3322)

CORE review: graphics. D3DKMTCreateKeyedMutex(2), OpenKeyedMutex(2),
OpenKeyedMutexFromNtHandle and DestroyKeyedMutex were stubs, and
D3DKMTAcquireKeyedMutex(2) and D3DKMTReleaseKeyedMutex(2) did not exist.

A keyed mutex is a block in a section like a resource (3321): its key and
owner are in the header, so every process that has it open sees the same
mutex. A global share handle or an NT handle (D3DKMTShareObjects, alone or
together with the resource it belongs to, whose block it then becomes part of;
D3DKMTOpenResourceFromNtHandle gives it back as hKeyedMutex) opens it in
another process. Acquire waits for the key (a timeout in the NT convention),
Release hands the mutex over with the next key and fails for anybody but the
owner; a mutex whose owner object is destroyed or whose owner process is gone
is abandoned (STATUS_ABANDONED_WAIT_0), as DXGI documents for IDXGIKeyedMutex.

New syscalls: win32u.spec, gdi32.spec, ntgdi.h, main.c, the wow64 thunks and
win32syscalls.h (regenerated with tools/make_specfiles: every later syscall
number moves, a merge with another patch that adds one needs a regeneration).
wow64win: CreateKeyedMutex2 did not return hSharedHandle, the Open variants not
the runtime data size.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XZFS4SPPzeH5d2c1MuzqCS


Rebased at integration (integ-2026-10) onto 5203 (stubs-6c), 3753 (fast-b): win32syscalls.h regenerated with tools/make_specfiles (syscall ids after 5203's and 3753's).
